oh  these are the faces of new patients  who've contacted Sky News since our  initial investigation independent of one  another 30 more youngsters have come  forward once again they say they were  failed by the care they received  they all went to mental health units run  by a single provider the hanticum group  now run by Active Care Group I thought I  went there to be kept safe  amongst those who got in touch are Tyson  Vickers and his mother Rachel I could  see that he was getting a lot worse we  were seeing much more self-harm how  would you describe Tyson when he went in  compared to how he was when he came out  he had cuts on his arms he was bandaged  up on both arms I mean he looked like  he'd been in a car crash  sixteen-year-old Tyson is autistic his  two months at the maidenhead unit in  March and April this year is not  something that's easy to talk about  like I still have experience like  flashbacks of things that happened there  what kinds of things like being  restrained  or like I end  um that's injection medication yeah I  was told by one staff member I would  never get out that I was just gonna be  like stuck there forever because I I  couldn't get help that's the first time  I've heard you say that  we gathered more than 20 testimonies for  our original investigation broadcast  nearly two months ago it raised concerns  over issues such as over-restrained and  allegations of inadequate supervision  leaving patients at increased risk of  self-harm  some of the 30 new people we've spoken  to shed these images this former patient  told us she got bruised Knuckles and  legs during restraints another took  these pictures of Blood on the walls she  says she was left alone for hours to  self-harm Ami told us when her underwear  was removed so she could be put into  anti-ligator clothing there was a male  member of Staff in the room I was  embarrassed she said it really went past  all my boundaries  another patient who didn't want to be  identified said her time at the  maidenhead unit left her traumatized  with walking difficulties and ticks she  told us of her life now I cannot live  independently  separate to our investigation we've  learned the first steps have been taken  by solicitor Mark McGee towards legal  action against the hanticum group he's  working on nine cases including the  family of a young patient who claims  they were raped by a member of staff at  the maidenhead unit Thames Valley police  are investigating this is systemic  systemic failure and its gross systemic  neglect all of these individuals have  been profoundly affected in terms of the  abuse that they've sustained and it was  it is going to affect the rest of their  lives Active Care Group took over the  hauntigam group in December 2021 a  spokesperson said we are very sad and  concerned to hear about these patient  experiences and allegations of poor care  a handful of which relate to time in our  care our policies and clinical  interventions are in line with national  guidelines and best practice  the previous owner's Ellie Investments  Group said it regrets Specialist Care  Services failed to meet the expected  standards for high quality Care NHS  England says in recent weeks current  patients have been spoken to and where  necessary action will be taken Lisa  Holland Sky News

